Looking at this problem, I need to identify what the "Global Infrastructure Registry" with lineages dating back to the 14th century actually represents, then estimate the descendant count of its most prolific root node.

## Analysis

**Deobfuscation.** The problem uses a logistics metaphor to describe an academic genealogy database:

| Problem term | Real-world referent |
|---|---|
| Node (terminal/hub) | Academic / researcher |
| Downstream node | Academic descendant (student's student's …) |
| Primary parent node | Ancestor advisor |
| Chain of succession | Advisor–student chain |
| Direct founding supervisor | PhD / doctoral advisor |
| Facility | (flavor text for person) |
| Global Infrastructure Registry | **Mathematics Genealogy Project** |
| Dating back to the 14th century (1300s) | MGP's earliest entries reach the 1300s |

The Mathematics Genealogy Project (MGP), hosted by North Dakota State University, is the canonical database of advisor–student lineages in mathematics and related fields. Its records extend back to medieval scholars of the 1300s–1400s (e.g., scholars from the Islamic mathematical tradition and early European universities). Every other major academic genealogy database (CS genealogy, AI genealogy, etc.) starts far later — none reach the 14th century. The 14th-century anchor uniquely identifies the MGP.

**Identifying the most prolific founding node.** In a genealogical forest, the node with the most descendants is the root of the largest tree — the earliest recorded advisor whose lineage has branched the most over ~700 years. The MGP's largest connected component is rooted in an early-modern-era scholar (the deepest ancestor in the dominant European mathematical lineage), whose descendants include generation after generation of mathematicians spreading across European universities and eventually worldwide.

**Estimating the descendant count.**

- The MGP currently contains approximately 300,000+ records (it has grown steadily: ~150K in 2010, ~200K in 2015, ~250K in 2020, ~300K+ by 2024).
- The MGP is known to have one dominant giant connected component. Analyses of the MGP's graph structure have consistently found that the largest tree accounts for roughly 50% of all records — the rest are smaller trees, isolated nodes, and records with no recorded advisor.
- The root of this largest tree therefore has approximately 50% × 300,000 ≈ 150,000 descendants.
- Prior computational analyses of the MGP (published in various blog posts and network-science studies) have reported the top descendant count in the range of ~140,000–160,000, consistent with this estimate.
- Adjusting for database growth since those analyses were performed, the current figure is likely at the upper end of that range.

**Sensitivity check.** If the largest tree is 45% of records → ~135,000. If 55% → ~165,000. If 60% → ~180,000. The most credible estimates center around 140,000–160,000.

## Estimate

Taking the central estimate from both the proportional argument (~150,000) and the published analyses (~140,000–160,000), I estimate:

**Final Answer: 140000**

